reps: 8
vscode: Ctrl+Tab back to the other editor, then scroll to find the line again
open: order.repository.ts order.entity.ts
vimrc: set hidden

@brief
Eight times: the `Order` entity gets a `placedAt: Date;` field under
`total`, and `toEntity` in the repository gets `placedAt: record.placedAt,`
under its `total` line.

Start in the repository. Find the `total` line, switch to the entity,
find its `total` line, add the field. Then come back to the repository
line you were on without searching for it again.

@keys
| `:bn` | next buffer |
| `Ctrl-O` | back one jump; twice here, because the search in the entity was one |
| `o` | open a line below and type |
| `:wqa` | write both, quit |

@hints
Search, switch buffer, search, edit. That is three jumps out, and the
first of the three is the one you want to land on.
--
`Ctrl-O` `Ctrl-O`: the first one undoes the entity search and lands at
the top of the entity, the second crosses back into the repository on
the `total` line.

@solution
    /total<Enter> :bn<Enter> /total<Enter> oplacedAt: Date;<Esc>
    Ctrl-O Ctrl-O oplacedAt: record.placedAt,<Esc> :wqa<Enter>

The pattern to make automatic: search, switch, search, edit, `Ctrl-O`
`Ctrl-O`, edit. Adding a field to an entity and its mapper is something
you will do hundreds of times in a service like this, and it is always
the same two-file hop.

If the double `Ctrl-O` bothers you, notice why it is double: the search
inside the entity was a jump. Had you moved to `total` with `4j`
instead, one `Ctrl-O` would have done it. Either is fine. What is not
fine is `:bp` followed by scrolling to find your line again.

@start order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public async findByCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: { customerId: args.customerId, deleted: null },
    });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
      cancellationReason: record.cancellationReason ?? undefined,
    };
  }
}

@expected order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';
import { Order as OrderRecord } from '@prisma/client';
import { BaseRepository } from '../../infrastructure/base.repository';
import { Order } from './order.entity';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository extends BaseRepository<OrderRecord, Order> {
  public async findByCustomer(args: { customerId: string }) {
    const records = await this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: { customerId: args.customerId, deleted: null },
    });

    return records.map((record) => this.toEntity(record));
  }

  public toEntity(record: OrderRecord): Order {
    return {
      id: record.id,
      customerId: record.customerId,
      status: record.status,
      total: record.total,
      placedAt: record.placedAt,
      cancellationReason: record.cancellationReason ?? undefined,
    };
  }
}

@start order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';

@expected order.entity.ts
export type Order = {
  id: string;
  customerId: string;
  status: OrderStatus;
  total: number;
  placedAt: Date;
  cancellationReason?: string;
};

export type OrderStatus = 'PENDING' | 'SHIPPED' | 'CANCELLED';
